
# make it argument sensitive

Arguments do not create separate instances: the same executable in the same directory is one instance.

# keep window on exit option
	 always eep window

# display error if process doesnt start

Session exists? tmux has-session -t myapp — exit 0 means the window is still alive.
Process alive? grab #{pane_pid} and kill -0 "$pid". This works even if the pane has already closed, unlike list-panes.
Exit code? needs remain-on-exit, otherwise the window is destroyed the instant the command exits and you have no evidence at all.

The gotcha that cost me two attempts
remain-on-exit is a window option, and set-option -w -t myapp only affects the currently selected window — new windows won't inherit it, so a failing program's window still vanishes. Set it globally after a session exists (setting it before fails, since a session-less server exits immediately):

bash
Copy
tmux new-session -d -s myapp -n main 'sleep 300'
tmux set-option -g remain-on-exit on      # now new windows survive death
Without remain-on-exit, wrap the command to record the status — verified working, produced EXIT:7 while the window itself disappeared:

bash
Copy
tmux new-window -t myapp -n job "sh -c 'sleep 0.3; exit 7'; echo \"EXIT:\$?\" > /tmp/job.status"
To read a dead pane's earlier output, add scrollback: tmux capture-pane -p -S -100 -t myapp:main (plain -p shows only the visible screen, which for a dead pane can be just the "Pane is dead" line).

One caveat about where this applies: these run inside this chat's cloud VM. Backgrounding a shell command with & inside a run_terminal_command call is not a substitute — tmux is the right tool precisely because the daemon outlives the call.

All test sessions were killed afterward; nothing is left running.





